ZTEXT00100002684deeac0ae0000000
Zero OS loader implementation notes - AMD64 UEFI ABI

This file describes loader.zero and the x86-64 ABI. loader32.zero uses
different pointer widths, structure offsets and calling conventions documented
in UEFI32.ztext. Compile the AMD64 loader with the unified compiler:
build/zero --efi < loader.zero > build/BOOTX64.EFI

Both loaders use the shared 256-byte u64 handoff described in
UEFI-CONSOLE.ztext, including execution architecture at offset 104 and owned
stack-pool base/size at offsets112/120, native compiler bundle base/size at
128/136 and workspace base/size at144/152.

Reference interfaces only; no implementation copied
https://uefi.org/specs/UEFI/2.10/04_EFI_System_Table.html
https://uefi.org/specs/UEFI/2.10/07_Services_Boot_Services.html
https://uefi.org/specs/UEFI/2.10/09_Protocols_EFI_Loaded_Image.html
https://uefi.org/specs/UEFI/2.10/12_Protocols_Console_Support.html
https://uefi.org/specs/UEFI/2.10/13_Protocols_Media_Access.html
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/debug/pe-format

Widths and status
Offsets below are bytes with AMD64 natural alignment and 8-byte pointers.
EFI_STATUS is u64; bit 63 denotes an error. Zero is EFI_SUCCESS, while positive
nonzero values are warnings. The loader requires exact success before using
outputs; warnings never stand for internal completion.
EFI_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL=8000000000000005.
EFI_INVALID_PARAMETER=8000000000000002.

System table and boot-service offsets
Entry RCX is ImageHandle; RDX points to EFI_SYSTEM_TABLE.
SystemTable: Header0..23, FirmwareVendor24, FirmwareRevision32,
ConsoleInHandle40, ConIn48, ConsoleOutHandle56, ConOut64,
StandardErrorHandle72, StdErr80, RuntimeServices88, BootServices96,
NumberOfTableEntries104, ConfigurationTable112.

BootServices: AllocatePages40, FreePages48, GetMemoryMap56, AllocatePool64,
FreePool72, HandleProtocol152, ExitBootServices232, SetWatchdogTimer256,
LocateProtocol320, CopyMem352, SetMem360. The reserved slot at160 must be
counted. In current Zero syntax a five-argument ABI operation has this form:
:call[@function | @a1 | @a2 | @a3 | @a4 | @a5] -> @status.

Loaded image and filesystem
LoadedImage GUID: 5B1B31A1-9562-11D2-8E3F-00A0C969723B.
GUID memory: u32 5b1b31a1, u16 9562, u16 11d2,
then bytes 8e 3f 00 a0 c9 69 72 3b.
HandleProtocol receives ImageHandle, GUID address and output-pointer address.
LoadedImage offsets: Revision0, ParentHandle8, SystemTable16, DeviceHandle24,
FilePath32, Reserved40, LoadOptionsSize48, LoadOptions56, ImageBase64,
ImageSize72, ImageCodeType80, ImageDataType84, Unload88. Total size 96.

SimpleFileSystem GUID: 964E5B22-6459-11D2-8E39-00A0C969723B.
Revision is at0; OpenVolume at8. Obtain the protocol through the loaded
image's DeviceHandle. OpenVolume receives the protocol and root-output
pointer. The AMD64 loader opens UTF-16 \kernel.bin from that root. IA32 opens
\kernel32.bin through its own ABI; the shared FAT image contains both.

EFI_FILE_PROTOCOL: Revision0, Open8, Close16, Delete24, Read32, Write40,
GetPosition48, SetPosition56, GetInfo64, SetInfo72, Flush80.
Open receives root, new-file-output, UTF16 path, mode1 and attributes0.
Read receives file, byte-count-pointer and destination. It can return fewer
bytes than requested; zero progress is rejected before kernel completion.
Close the file and root before final memory-map acquisition.

EFI_FILE_INFO GUID: 09576E92-6D3F-11D2-8E39-00A0C969723B.
Layout: Size0, FileSize8, PhysicalSize16 (u64); EFI_TIME fields24/40/56;
Attribute72; UTF16 FileName80. Bounds/status must be validated before outputs
are used. Successful GetInfo must report80..512 bytes before FileSize is read.
The loader rejects an empty or larger-than491520-byte kernel and accumulates
validated partial reads with a separate completion flag.

Allocation
AllocatePages receives allocation type, memory type, page count and output
physical-address pointer. Types0/1/2 mean any/max/fixed address; pages are 4096
bytes. EfiLoaderCode=1 holds executable kernel pages; EfiLoaderData=2 holds
stack, handoff and memory map. Initialize output storage and check status
before using it; do not assume a fixed physical address is free.
The AMD64 loader allocates120 code pages (480KiB),256 stack-pool pages (1MiB) and
17 handoff/map pages. The main entry uses the pool's upper end; its base and
size are handed to the kernel for two cooperative worker stacks. The handoff
is explicitly cleared for 256 bytes. Its final map buffer is 64 KiB.
IA32 uses bounded below-4 GiB allocations
described in UEFI32.ztext.

GOP
GraphicsOutput GUID: 9042A9DE-23DC-4A38-96FB-7ADED080516A.
GOP methods: QueryMode0, SetMode8, Blt16, Mode24.
Mode: MaxMode0, Mode4, Info8, SizeOfInfo16, FrameBufferBase24,
FrameBufferSize32.
Info: Version0, HorizontalResolution4, VerticalResolution8, PixelFormat12,
RedMask16, GreenMask20, BlueMask24, ReservedMask28, PixelsPerScanLine32.
Pixel formats are RGB0, BGR1, bit-mask2 and BltOnly3. The kernel supports the
first two 32-bit layouts. Stride is PixelsPerScanLine, not visible width.
Validate geometry, stride, size and pointer arithmetic, then copy metadata
before exit. UEFI need not expose legacy VGA text memory at B8000.
The loader prefers an eligible1920x1080 mode, retains800x600/640x480 or another
compatible fallback, then validates the actual current
mode even if selection failed. See UEFI-CONSOLE.ztext for implemented mode
selection and shared handoff.

Exit sequence
1. Disable the firmware watchdog and retain any failure status.
2. Load the complete kernel, allocate its stack and handoff/map storage,
   establish display metadata, release queried mode buffers, close files.
3. Restore map capacity and obtain size, key, descriptor stride and version.
   Validate that returned data fits the preallocated buffer and at least one
   descriptor fits; stride must be at least 40 bytes.
4. Immediately call ExitBootServices with the acquired key. No intervening
   firmware console, allocation, protocol or file operation is allowed.
5. Retry only EFI_INVALID_PARAMETER, acquiring a fresh map each time. At
   most four exit attempts are made. After the first attempt, only the two
   map/exit services are used; diagnostics are direct UART output.
6. On success, !enter[@entry | @handoff | @stack_top]. disables maskable
   interrupts, sets the dedicated stack and jumps to the kernel. It never
   returns to firmware. On failure, a bounded diagnostic path halts.

EFI_MEMORY_DESCRIPTOR minimum fields: Type0, PhysicalStart8, VirtualStart16,
NumberOfPages24, Attribute32. Actual stride comes from firmware and can
exceed 40. The descriptor-version field is 32 bits; the cleared handoff leaves
its upper32 bits zero.

Microsoft x64 call ABI
First four integer/pointer arguments use RCX/RDX/R8/R9; remaining arguments
are on the stack. Before CALL, RSP is 16-byte aligned and 32 shadow bytes are
reserved. The compiler's five-argument primitive subtracts 48 bytes and places
argument 5 at [RSP+32]; the callee sees it at [RSP+40].
RAX is the result. RCX/RDX/R8-R11 and XMM0-5 are volatile; RBX/RBP/RDI/RSI/RSP/
R12-R15 are nonvolatile. The compiler preserves its previous RSP in R12
across the conforming firmware call, and protects nested expression values.
Entry RCX/RDX are captured before the generic program frame is initialized.

PE32+ image contract
DOS header has MZ and e_lfanew at60; PE signature is at offset 64.
COFF machine8664, two sections, optional-header size 240, characteristics22.
Optional magic20b, entry RVA1000, ImageBase100000, section/file alignment
4096/512, EFI subsystem10 and 16 data directories. Header size 512.
.text starts at RVA1000/file offset 512; characteristics60000020.
.reloc follows aligned code; characteristics42000040, virtual size 12, raw
size 512. The base-relocation directory points to a valid no-op ABSOLUTE block.
Generated code is position-independent and has no import table or runtime.
All section/directory RVAs are relative to ImageBase. IA32 PE32 uses its
separate machine/header values in X86.ztext, not these AMD64 values.

Historical 0.4.0 verification
Matching AMD64 and IA32 OVMF firmware are external testing dependencies.
The final 0.4.0 ISO reached both UEFI kernels and their visible terminals in
QEMU, with PS/2 and shared-disk persistence checks. Exact release hashes,
commands, logs and remaining limitations for the current release are in
verification.ztext. Neither
native VirtualBox nor physical-hardware operation is established by that test.

Historical384KiB growth validation is recorded in
verification/boot-growth-0.7.0/results.ztext. Both OVMF architectures completed
the full-slot tail/arena/pool probe after ExitBootServices. The probe checks
markers across the128KiB boundaries and at final byte393215; it does not
infer a complete load merely from reaching the first kernel instructions.

The historical 448 KiB capacity-only probes remain in
verification/boot-capacity-448k/. Current 480 KiB slots and the native compiler
bundle are checked in verification/guest-bundle-480k/; the probe verifies the
whole 1 MiB loaded bundle, every 8 MiB workspace page and the final kernel
byte on BIOS/UEFI, both 32- and 64-bit. Native compiler execution evidence is
separate in verification/guest-app/bundle/. Final OS integration results
remain separate in verification.ztext.

Native guest compilers
Both UEFI loaders load an exact1MiB TOOLS.BIN from the boot filesystem and
validate ZRBNDL01/version1/size1048576/count6. They allocate256 LoaderCode pages
for it and2048 LoaderCode pages (8MiB) for the compiler workspace and generated
programs. These are separate from kernel code, stack and the handoff/map.
IA32 allocations retain explicit below4GiB limits. The files are closed before
GetMemoryMap/ExitBootServices; no runtime compiler accesses firmware. Bundle
construction and six app slots are documented in portability/guest/BUNDLE.ztext.
